>> title: Gyoza
>> category: Dumplings & Rolls
>> tags: pork, japanese, dumpling, pan-fry, cabbage
>> counters: Ramen Shop, Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: gyoza, gyōza, 餃子, pot stickers, potstickers, yaki gyoza, yaki-gyoza, fried dumplings, pork dumplings, an order of six
>> pairs-with: shoyu-ramen, tonkotsu-ramen
>> servings: 4
>> time: 1 hr 45 min
>> slack: narrow — the cabbage is salted and wrung until almost nothing more comes, and that is the step that decides everything: wet filling steams grey inside a wrapper that then splits. The pan is the other window, three minutes to set the base before the water goes in
>> step.1: A gyoza is not a jiaozi: the wrapper is thinner, the filling is wrung dry, and it is pleated on one side only so it sits flat in the pan.
>> step.2: knead 8 min to a firm dough
>> step.3: rest 30 min under a bowl
>> step.4: salt 20 min, then wring dry
>> step.5: beat one way 3 min until sticky
>> step.6: roll thin, cut 3.5-in rounds
>> step.7: fill, pleat one side, press flat
>> step.8: fry 3 min, steam 6 min, crisp 2 min

A gyoza is not a jiaozi: the wrapper is thinner, the filling is wrung dry, and it is pleated on one side only so it sits flat in the pan. Everything about it is built around the fact that one face gets fried and the rest gets steamed.

Knead @all-purpose flour{2%cups}(260 g), @fine sea salt{1/2%tsp}(3 g) and @boiling water{2/3%cup}(160 mL) together ~knead{8%min}, until it is firm and smooth and springs back slowly. Boiling water is deliberate — it scalds the starch, and a hot-water dough rolls out thin without fighting back.

Rest @&(~1)dough{} under an upturned bowl ~rest{30%min}.

Salt @napa cabbage{3%cups}(225 g; chopped fine) with @kosher salt{1%tsp}(5 g) and ~drain{20%min}, then take it up in a cloth and wring it out until almost nothing more comes. This is the step people skip and it is the one that decides everything: wet cabbage steams the filling grey inside a wrapper that then splits.

Beat @ground pork{8%oz}(225 g), @garlic chives{1/2%cup}(30 g; chopped), @garlic{2%cloves}(grated), @fresh ginger{1%Tbs}(grated), @soy sauce{1%Tbs}(15 mL), @toasted sesame oil{2%tsp}(10 mL), @sake{1%Tbs}(15 mL), @granulated sugar{1%tsp}(4 g) and @white pepper{1/4%tsp} into @&(~1)wrung cabbage{} ~beat{3%min}, stirring in one direction only until the mass turns tacky and holds a fingerprint.

Roll @&(~3)rested dough{} out thin on @cornstarch{2%Tbs}(20 g) and cut it into 3 1/2-inch rounds, working each round thinner at the edge than the middle.

Fill @&(~1)wrappers{} with a scant tablespoon of @&(~2)filling{} each, wet the rim, and pleat the near side only — six or seven folds, all facing the same way — then stand each one up and press the base flat on the board.

Fry @&(~1)gyoza{} flat-side-down in @neutral oil{1%Tbs}(15 mL) in a lidded #skillet{} ~fry{3%min} until the bases are set and gold, pour in @water{1/3%cup}(80 mL), cover at once and ~steam{6%min}, then take the lid off and let the last of the water go ~fry{2%min} until the base crackles when you slide a spatula under it. Turn them out onto a plate crust-side up.
